      <p>Dental searches range from the urgent to the carefully planned. A cracked tooth on a Saturday, a family looking for a new practice and a patient researching implants for months are all searching for a dentist, but they are worth very different amounts and behave very differently. Google Ads works when the account treats them that way.</p>

      <h2>Three campaigns for three kinds of patient</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Emergency dentistry:</strong> toothache, broken teeth, swelling. Urgent and call-driven, won by availability and answering the phone</li>
        <li><strong>General and new patients:</strong> check-ups, hygiene, family dentistry. Steady volume, and the start of a long patient relationship</li>
        <li><strong>Implants and high-value treatment:</strong> implants, full-arch treatment, cosmetic work. Long decisions, high value, and their own campaign</li>
      </ul>
      <p>When everything shares one budget, cheap check-up clicks use money that implant searches need, and implant keywords bid like routine appointments.</p>

      <h2>Implants need a different approach</h2>
      <p>Implant patients rarely book on their first visit to a website. They research what the treatment involves, what it costs, how long it takes and whether finance is available, then compare clinics.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Ads that offer a consultation rather than pushing a price</li>
        <li>Landing pages that explain the process, typical cost ranges and finance options honestly</li>
        <li>Proof patients can check: clinician qualifications, reviews, real cases where permitted</li>
        <li>Tracking that follows a consultation through to an accepted treatment plan</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Measured on form fills alone, implant campaigns look expensive. Measured on accepted treatment, they are often the most profitable part of the account.</p>

      <h2>Work within health advertising rules</h2>
      <p>Dental ads fall under Google's healthcare policies. In particular, Google restricts personalised advertising around health conditions and treatments, so some audience types, including certain remarketing uses, are not allowed for health-related ads. Check what is permitted for your ads and country before building audiences.</p>
      <p>Privacy rules matter too. Pages where patients book appointments or share health details need careful handling of tracking, and in some countries specific health privacy laws apply. Agree the tracking approach with whoever handles compliance for the practice.</p>

      <h2>Be available when emergencies search</h2>
      <p>Emergency dental searches spike in the evenings and at weekends. If the practice cannot see emergency patients then, the ads should not run then. Where it can, ads that state same-day or weekend availability, with a call button, win the patient.</p>

      <h2>Measure patients, not enquiries</h2>
      <p>A call is not a patient. Some callers want prices only, some need treatment you do not offer, some are suppliers. Feed real booked appointments and accepted treatment back from the practice management system, and give implant cases a higher value than check-ups. Then bidding learns which searches bring patients worth having. The <a href="/tools/metrics-calculator/">metrics calculator</a> helps set what you can afford per booking.</p>

      <h2>Where dental budgets leak</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Dental nurse, hygienist and dentist job searches</li>
        <li>Free or charity dental care, if you do not provide it</li>
        <li>At-home whitening kits and dental products</li>
        <li>Implant searches from people looking abroad for cheaper treatment</li>
        <li>Areas further away than patients will travel</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Where to start</h2>
      <p>If the practice already advertises, the first question is whether the account knows which enquiries became patients and treatment. A <a href="/google-ads-audit/">$200 account analysis</a> checks tracking and how the budget splits between emergency, general and implant work. <a href="#enquiry">Ask for a free audit</a> and tell me which treatments you most want to grow.</p>
